<p>Custer spent much of his childhood with a half-sister and her husband in Monroe, Michigan. He graduated from Hopedale Normal College in Ohio in 1856 at age 16, helping pay his expenses by hauling coal for the school, and then taught in Ohio.</p>

<p>His ambition was a military career. Appointed to West Point in 1857, Custer nearly faced expulsion several times because of excessive demerits, many earned for pranks. He graduated last in the 34-member class of 1861, reduced in size when cadets loyal to the South left to join the Confederacy.</p>

<p>Ordinarily, such a record would have led to an obscure assignment, but the Civil War created an urgent need for Union officers. Commissioned a second lieutenant in the Second Cavalry, Custer served on the staffs of Generals George McClellan and Alfred Pleasonton and repeatedly distinguished himself. On June 29, 1863, after gallant conduct at Aldie, he rose from first lieutenant to brevet brigadier general. At 23, he was among the youngest generals in U.S. military history.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
