    <figcaption>George and Elizabeth Custer, 1864</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Custer met {% person "elizabeth-bacon-custer" %}Elizabeth Clift Bacon{% endperson %} in Monroe in 1862. Her father, Judge Daniel Stanton Bacon, initially opposed their marriage because he wanted more security for his daughter than life with an Army officer from a lower social station. He relented after Custer's promotion, and the couple married February 9, 1864. Libbie accompanied the Army closely, living in tents near battlefields, riding with soldiers, and joining Seventh Cavalry expeditions.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/george-armstrong-custer/180-200px-gengacuster.jpg" alt="Brevet Major General George Armstrong Custer in 1865">
    <figcaption>Brevet Major General Custer, 1865</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Custer commanded the Michigan Cavalry Brigade during the Gettysburg campaign. His characteristic “Custer Dash” was regarded as both reckless and brilliant. He continued fighting with distinction and became a brevet major general at age 25. Those senior wartime ranks were temporary, however, and after the war he returned to his permanent rank of captain in the Fifth Cavalry.</p>

<p>Custer considered becoming a general in Benito Juárez's Mexican army, but the U.S. government denied his request for leave. After his father-in-law died in 1866, he returned to Monroe, considered a congressional campaign, and advocated moderate treatment of the former Confederacy.</p>

<p>After initially declining command of the Tenth Cavalry, a Black regiment known as Buffalo Soldiers, Custer accepted appointment as lieutenant colonel of the Seventh Cavalry at Fort Riley, Kansas. In 1867, an unauthorized absence to visit his wife led to a one-year suspension. General Philip Sheridan ended it early so Custer could join the Cheyenne campaign. Custer then attacked Chief Black Kettle's village at the Battle of the Washita.</p>

<p>After two years of recovery in Kentucky, the Seventh Cavalry was stationed at {% place "fort-abraham-lincoln" %}Fort Abraham Lincoln{% endplace %} in 1873. Cheyenne oral history says Custer entered a marriage ceremony with Monahsetah, daughter of Chief Little Rock, perhaps without understanding its purpose. She later bore a son attributed to Custer, though the child died before adulthood.</p>

