    <figcaption>Lieutenant Colonel Custer, 1875</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Custer was described as tall, slender, energetic, and dashing, with blue eyes, long golden hair, and a mustache. At the fort he wore velveteen uniforms with gold braid; in the field, he favored buckskins. He rarely drank or smoked and studied military history and tactics in his spare time.</p>

<p>In 1874, Custer led a government expedition, including Smithsonian scientists, into the Black Hills to investigate reports of gold. The 1868 Fort Laramie Treaty had reserved those lands for the Sioux. Confirmation brought an influx of miners and intensified conflict.</p>

<p>Custer was expected to lead the 1876 campaign against the Sioux and Cheyenne but was summoned to Washington to testify about Indian Bureau corruption. His criticism of Secretary of War William Belknap angered President Ulysses S. Grant, who removed him from command. Public pressure and Army support led Grant to reverse the decision.</p>

<p>The government sought to take control of the Black Hills goldfields and confine remaining Indigenous groups to reservations. The Seventh Cavalry left Fort Lincoln on May 17, 1876. Lakota, Northern Cheyenne, and Arapaho forces defeated it at the Battle of the Little Bighorn on June 25, killing Custer.</p>

<p>Custer's distinguished Civil War record was overshadowed by his role and death at the Little Bighorn, and debate continues over the decisions made by him and others. At the time of his death, the Democratic Party was reportedly considering the Civil War hero and Indian Wars commander as a presidential candidate.</p>

<p>Custer initially received a military funeral and burial at the battlefield cemetery. His remains were recovered in 1877 and reinterred at the U.S. Military Academy at West Point, where Elizabeth was later buried beside him.</p>
